/*
==============================================================================

FROXEL INTEGRATE - turn per-cell scattering into a lookup table.

One thread per screen COLUMN of the volume; each walks its 64 slices front to
back and accumulates.  After this pass, cell (x,y,z) holds:

    rgb  total in-scattered radiance between the eye and the FAR face of slice z
    a    transmittance from the eye to that same face

so a consumer needs a single 3D texture fetch at its own depth instead of a
march.  That is the whole point of the grid: the expensive work happened in
froxel_scatter.comp, and what is left is a prefix sum.

The analytic integration below is the standard one for a homogeneous slab, and
it matters.  Naively accumulating `scatter * thickness * transmittance` is a
left Riemann sum, and the slices here are exponentially distributed - the far
ones are hundreds of units thick - so that sum badly over-counts wherever the
medium is thick enough to absorb within a single slice.  In practice it reads as
distant fog that is far too bright.  Integrating the slab exactly removes the
dependence on slice thickness entirely.

==============================================================================
*/

#version 460
#extension GL_GOOGLE_include_directive : enable
// god_rays_shared.h declares the fog TLAS; this pass never traces, but the type
// has to be known for the header to parse - the same note god_rays_filter.comp
// carries.
#extension GL_EXT_ray_query : enable

#define GOD_RAYS_DESC_SET_IDX 0
#include "god_rays_shared.h"

#define GLOBAL_UBO_DESC_SET_IDX 2
#include "global_ubo.h"

#include "froxel_shared.h"

// 0 and 1 on this set are the shadow map and the fog TLAS (god_rays_shared.h).
layout(set = GOD_RAYS_DESC_SET_IDX, binding = 2, rgba16f) uniform image3D IMG_FROXEL_SCATTER;
layout(set = GOD_RAYS_DESC_SET_IDX, binding = 4, rgba16f) uniform image3D IMG_FROXEL_INTEGRATED;

/*
=================
load_scatter

One cell of the scatter volume, optionally with a 3x3 blur across the SLICE.

WHY THIS IS HERE AND NOT A PASS OF ITS OWN. RTX Remix runs a dedicated
volume_filter between its integrate and preintegrate steps. The same work fits
inside this pass for free: it already walks every cell exactly once, so the
filter costs eight extra imageLoads per cell and needs no third volume, no
second dispatch and no extra barrier.

WHY IT IS NEEDED AT ALL. Every cell of the scatter volume is ONE light sample
with ONE binary visibility ray. RIS makes the chosen light a good one, but
visibility stays all-or-nothing and cannot be importance-sampled away: a cell
whose light happened to be occluded is black next to a cell whose light was not.
That disagreement is the remaining blotchiness, and no number of RIS candidates
touches it - it is the one factor the target function cannot include, because
knowing it is what the ray is for.

BLURRING WITHIN A SLICE IS GEOMETRICALLY SOUND. Every cell of a given z sits at
the same view depth, so neighbours are genuinely adjacent in the medium and there
is no depth discontinuity to smear across - unlike a screen-space blur, which is
why this can be a plain Gaussian with no bilateral weighting.

IT NOW BLURS ALONG Z TOO, which an earlier version refused to do on the grounds
that the far slices are hundreds of units thick. That reasoning had the wrong
measure: what matters is the RATIO, and the slices are exponentially spaced at a
constant ratio of about 1.13, so one slice either way is always the same ~13% of
depth whether it is 2 units or 900. Physically the medium varies smoothly over
that, and refusing the third axis threw away two thirds of the samples the
neighbourhood could offer. 27 taps rather than 9, for a ~1.7x cut in noise at no
extra ray cost - and the noise that remains is dominated by the two BINARY rays
per cell (the light's visibility and getSkyVisibility), which is exactly the kind
of all-or-nothing error a wider neighbourhood averages out.

The kernel is the separable 1-2-1 binomial in all three axes. Edges clamp, which
double-weights the border cells; in x and y they are off the edge of the screen
anyway, and in z they are the near and far planes.
=================
*/
vec4 load_scatter(ivec2 column, int z)
{
	// Undo the store-time pre-multiply, so the integration below works in the
	// medium's true per-unit-length units - the exp(-sigma * thickness) needs the
	// real extinction, not a scaled one. See froxel_shared.h.
	// Undo the store-time pre-multiply, so the integration below works in the
	// medium's true per-unit-length units - the exp(-sigma * thickness) needs the
	// real extinction, not a scaled one. See froxel_shared.h.
	int radius = int(global_ubo.pt_fog_froxel_filter);

	if (radius <= 0)
		return imageLoad(IMG_FROXEL_SCATTER, ivec3(column, z))
		     * FROXEL_STORAGE_INV_SCALE;

	/* THE RADIUS IS THE MAIN NOISE KNOB THAT COSTS NO RAYS, which is what makes
	   it the right one to reach for first.

	   Every cell of the scatter volume is ONE light sample carrying TWO BINARY
	   rays - the chosen light's visibility and getSkyVisibility. Binary is the
	   one factor RIS cannot importance-sample away, because knowing it is what
	   the ray is for, so raising pt_fog_light_samples cannot touch it. What DOES
	   average it out is looking at more neighbours, and a neighbour is an
	   imageLoad, not a ray - so widening this is close to free next to anything
	   that traces.

	     0  off, one cell
	     1  3x3x3, 27 taps   - the long-standing behaviour and the default
	     2  5x5x5, 125 taps
	     3  7x7x7, 343 taps

	   Clamped at 3 because the tap count is cubic and past that the cost stops
	   being incidental. Raising it trades fog DETAIL for smoothness: the shafts
	   and the pools of glow around a fixture get softer, which is exactly the
	   structure the per-point sky trace exists to produce, so this is a taste
	   knob and not a free win.

	   The weight is the separable triangular kernel w(d) = radius + 1 - |d|,
	   which at radius 1 is 2,1 - i.e. bit-identical to the 1-2-1 binomial this
	   replaced, so the default renders exactly as before. */
	radius = min(radius, 3);

	vec4 sum = vec4(0);
	float wsum = 0.0;

	for (int dz = -radius; dz <= radius; dz++)
	{
		for (int dy = -radius; dy <= radius; dy++)
		{
			for (int dx = -radius; dx <= radius; dx++)
			{
				ivec3 c = clamp(ivec3(column, z) + ivec3(dx, dy, dz),
				                ivec3(0),
				                ivec3(FROXEL_GRID_X - 1, FROXEL_GRID_Y - 1, FROXEL_GRID_Z - 1));

				float w = float(radius + 1 - abs(dx))
				        * float(radius + 1 - abs(dy))
				        * float(radius + 1 - abs(dz));

				sum += imageLoad(IMG_FROXEL_SCATTER, c) * w;
				wsum += w;
			}
		}
	}

	return (sum / wsum) * FROXEL_STORAGE_INV_SCALE;
}

layout(local_size_x = FROXEL_INT_GROUP_X, local_size_y = FROXEL_INT_GROUP_Y, local_size_z = 1) in;
void main()
{
	ivec2 column = ivec2(gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy);

	if (column.x >= FROXEL_GRID_X || column.y >= FROXEL_GRID_Y)
		return;

	/* While a debug view is up, froxel_scatter has ALREADY written the diagnostic
	   straight into the integrated volume, and this pass must not overwrite it.

	   This pass used to do the display itself, by copying the scatter volume
	   through unaccumulated. That forced every debug view to stage its value in
	   the SCATTER volume - which is also the temporal HISTORY - so the views were
	   corrupting the very thing they were built to measure, and view 6 (the
	   history fetch) fed on its own output and went black regardless of the truth.
	   Bailing out here is what lets the scatter pass keep writing the genuine
	   result while a view is on screen. */
	if (global_ubo.pt_fog_froxel_debug > 0.0)
		return;

	vec3 accum_inscatter = vec3(0);
	float transmittance = 1.0;

	float near_depth = FROXEL_NEAR;

	for (int z = 0; z < FROXEL_GRID_Z; z++)
	{
		ivec3 cell = ivec3(column, z);

		vec4 slice = load_scatter(column, z);

		float far_depth = FROXEL_SLICE_TO_DEPTH(float(z) + 1.0);
		float thickness = max(far_depth - near_depth, 0.0);
		near_depth = far_depth;

		vec3 scatter = slice.rgb;      // radiance per unit length
		float sigma = max(slice.a, 0.0);   // extinction per unit length

		if (sigma > 1e-7)
		{
			// Exact integral of scatter * exp(-sigma * t) over [0, thickness],
			// which is scatter/sigma * (1 - exp(-sigma * thickness)). This is
			// the term that stops thick far slices from over-contributing.
			float slice_transmittance = exp(-sigma * thickness);
			vec3 slice_inscatter = (scatter / sigma) * (1.0 - slice_transmittance);

			accum_inscatter += slice_inscatter * transmittance;
			transmittance *= slice_transmittance;
		}
		else
		{
			// No meaningful extinction: the exponential collapses to the
			// straight product, and dividing by sigma above would blow up.
			accum_inscatter += scatter * thickness * transmittance;
		}

		imageStore(IMG_FROXEL_INTEGRATED, cell, vec4(accum_inscatter, transmittance));
	}
}
